---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

import Header from "../../components/shared/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/shared/Footer.astro";
import PostHeader from "../../components/blog/PostHeader.astro";

import "../../styles/blog/blog-post.css";
---

<Layout
    title="Learning how to code by using my own app"
    description="How building and using my own hiking app, Crestr, taught me to code."
>
    <Header
        links={[
            { label: "Home", href: "/" },
            { label: "All Articles", href: "/blog" },
        ]}
    />

    <main id="top">
        <article class="blog-post">
            <div class="container">
                <PostHeader
                    title="Learning how to code by using my own app"
                    date="22-11-2026"
                    tags={["Crestr", "Full-Stack", "Learning"]}
                />

                <div class="post-content">
                    <blockquote>
                        Crestr is an open-source hiking route planner for the UK
                        and Ireland that I built starting when I was 16 (2 years
                        ago at the time of writing). I currently maintain and
                        update the project.
                    </blockquote>

                    <h2>Why I started</h2>

                    <p>
                        I was my own prospective user, and so I had a good idea
                        of what Crestr needed: an app which let me make routes,
                        as well as import or export them for free, without a
                        subscription.
                    </p>

                    <h2>Starting out</h2>

                    <p>
                        What I didn't have however was the experience in
                        developing. I only knew how to make simple CLI tools in
                        Python, think calculators and basic quizzes. I had no
                        experience in databases, JS, Flask, or REST APIs, and
                        certainly none in building web apps. The project seemed
                        daunting, but given that it was A) a school project and
                        B) something that I would have loved to have, I
                        continued with it.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The result of brute-forcing my way into the project,
                        with very little experience and only YouTube videos, was
                        a project that continually evolved, from SQLite to
                        PostgreSQL, Flask to FastAPI, and JS to TS. However
                        every single change my codebase needed meant I learnt
                        more.
                    </p>

                    <h2>SQLite to PostgreSQL</h2>

                    <p>
                        One example is the migration from SQLite to PostgreSQL.
                        During early testing with friends, SQLite's one-writer
                        restriction stopped performance logging of routing. This
                        only surfaced with several users as I alone could never
                        create concurrent writes. This was important as the
                        logging was used to monitor issues in the routing
                        engine, such as spikes in routing times or routing times
                        that rise sharply with distance. One route is made up of
                        many segments, potentially 100+ depending on the length
                        of the route, and each segment wrote to the table.
                        Having multiple users each creating a route at the same
                        time meant that segment writes timed out (after 5s) with
                        a <code>SQLITE_BUSY</code> error as SQLite locked the
                        entire database. Migrating to PostgreSQL increased the
                        quantity of data available, as every write went through.
                        It also taught me that PostgreSQL locks at the row level
                        during writes, so concurrent writers don't block each
                        other.
                    </p>

                    <h2>Adopting TypeScript</h2>

                    <p>
                        Another example is the TypeScript migration. Using my
                        own app meant that the feedback loop from spotting a
                        missing feature to building it and testing it had no
                        delay, so features arrived in quick succession. This
                        took its toll on the frontend; to remember the type of
                        arguments a function needed, I either had to navigate
                        back and re-read it or trust a memory that faded with
                        each new feature. That led to bugs, and the compiler
                        caught 10+ of them after the migration alone.
                        Contributors who had worked on the project told me it
                        had become easier to add features, fix bugs and handle
                        small chores. It taught me the importance of not only
                        type-safety but also developer experience.
                    </p>

                    <h2>Splitting up the monofile</h2>

                    <p>
                        The last example is when I started Crestr's frontend
                        with a single JS and CSS file. It was fine for the first
                        month, but as with the TypeScript migration, the speed
                        of my feedback loop meant features were piled into the
                        file in quick succession. After just 6 months the
                        codebase had grown to around 2000 lines making
                        development speed slow. It also put off a potential
                        contributor who told me that the monolithic nature of
                        the file had made the project seem daunting, when it was
                        in fact quite logically simple; the size of the file was
                        the daunting aspect. Refactoring this taught me how to
                        structure a codebase and how modules worked and
                        interacted with each other.
                    </p>

                    <h2>Looking back</h2>

                    <p>
                        Looking back, building Crestr gave me skills that I
                        didn't think I'd have by my first year at university;
                        experience in full-stack web development, experience in
                        APIs and conversations with developers with many more
                        years of experience than me. Using my own app also
                        closed feedback loops with almost no delay, and whilst
                        that meant development was faster, it also meant that my
                        project grew faster than my experience could handle, and
                        so I had to restructure the project multiple times to
                        handle the influx of code. The cost was time; it took me
                        a long time to build Crestr to what it is now, even if
                        the time wasn't wasted as I spent it learning . The main
                        cause of that was the brute-force approach, as opposed
                        to taking time to build smaller side-projects with each
                        of the tools I went on to use. Regardless, I'd still
                        learn the same way; the real stakes of the project
                        forced me to fix the issues in it, whilst side projects
                        had no commitment to do so.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <footer class="post-footer">
                    <div class="post-footer-nav">
                        <a href="/blog" class="post-footer-link">
                            ← All Articles
                        </a>
                    </div>
                </footer>
            </div>
        </article>
    </main>
</Layout>
